<!DOCTYPE template>
<!--
  Description:
    One hotcue button, labelled with its number.

    Three states, as WHotcueButton expects: 0 empty, 1 set, 2 active. The
    number is on the button in every state, so an empty slot still says which
    one it is - on a panel this size there is no room for a legend elsewhere.

    <UseCueColor> follows the scheme. A multi-hue scheme lets a set hotcue show
    the colour the cue itself carries; a monochrome one turns that off, since
    the colour comes from the user's palette and has nothing to do with the
    scheme, and would be the only colour on the screen.

    Worth knowing when a set hotcue looks blank: with <UseCueColor> on, the
    cue's own colour wins over anything the stylesheet says, and a cue whose
    stored colour is black - which tracks carrying imported Serato or rekordbox
    markers often have - paints black on a black ground. That is the track's
    data, not the skin. A monochrome scheme, which switches the cue colour off,
    shows those cues normally.

  Variables:
    Group:  the deck, e.g. [Channel1]
    Number: 1..8
-->
<Template>
  <HotcueButton>
    <TooltipId>hotcue</TooltipId>
    <ObjectName>HotcueButton</ObjectName>
    <SizePolicy>me,f</SizePolicy>
    <Size>0me,44f</Size>
    <Group><Variable name="Group"/></Group>
    <Hotcue><Variable name="Number"/></Hotcue>
    <NumberStates>3</NumberStates>
    <DimBrightThreshold><Variable name="DimBrightThresholdHotcueBtn"/></DimBrightThreshold>
    <UseCueColor><Variable name="UseCueColor"/></UseCueColor>
    <State><Number>0</Number><Text><Variable name="Number"/></Text></State>
    <State><Number>1</Number><Text><Variable name="Number"/></Text></State>
    <State><Number>2</Number><Text><Variable name="Number"/></Text></State>
  </HotcueButton>
</Template>
